A||N||20||BellaFiero||Fiberglassing||||5||
Z||000000||BellaFiero||09-12-2000||12:05 PM||||Hey dudes and dudettes. Maybe you can help me out here... I had Fi-Air-O Scoops ( the twin over-the-roof and into-your-engine-vents style scoops ) made for my car and they look awesome. A little more blocky and sleek than what Conversions in Canada sells, but almost identical. Now, you've all seen the Diablo kit cars, etc. You know how the scoops over the roof go back into the rear of the car? They are much more sleek and angled, and I was just wondering what kind of price I would be looking at if I wanted someone to 'glass that effect to my car? How hard would it be to do, how much would I be looking at spending? <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=430291886" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=430291886</A> <BR>That's a link to a pic of a kitcar Lambo. I'm not looking to make a lambo kitcar here, just make the scoops look similiar. How hard, etc. Any thoughts or ideas on this would be appreciated. <P>Also - when I'm driving And I put my hand up through the opened top and feel the scoop, it feels like hot air is blowing OUT of the scoop! Any thoughts??<P>________<BR>Jayse's "Bella Fiero" Custom and 1985 GT "Cauldron Born" <P>------------------<BR>"No, no... you can't put that there, it'll be in the way of the Johnson Rod!"  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfixit58||09-12-2000||01:24 PM||||As mentioned many times before, the grills are designed to let air OUT (remember, heat rises). Your motor may actually run hotter using these scoops.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||BellaFiero||09-12-2000||01:37 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I know that! But the scoops feed directly onto the grills! Wouldn't the air be forced down into the grills and over the engine? I mean, I know the idea is purely an aesthetic visual, looking like an exotic with those rear scoops, but even the new Mr2s have scoops available to direct air into the engine compartment. Any way to make these more functional? That was what I considering the lambo scoops for - much bigger opening, easier path for air to get rammed.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||09-12-2000||01:49 PM||||Hey, if you find someone to make you those lambo scoops, I'll buy your Fi-Air-O scoops off ya!||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||09-12-2000||01:59 PM||||It won't matter how you have those scoops mounted. Air will ALWAYS be trying to get out. On the underside of the engine, below the muffler, is a rubber flap. This flap scoops air from under the car, circulates it around the engine, and it exits from the the louvers on top. This is the reason for the notchback design. It creates a low pressure zone directly behind the glass. The low pressure pulls the air out from under the decklid, allows for better air circulation, keeping the engine cool, which gives your engine a longer life. I wouldn't even consider putting anything over those vents on any Fiero I ever own.||||reg||1
Z||000005||BellaFiero||09-12-2000||02:05 PM||||Rick, your on the list for the scoops if I can get the lambo-esque scoops made for a notcback. Okay, here's the problem as I see it - alot of people have alot of opinions. And they're willing to back it up with facts. So here's my Opinion - I like the scoops. And here's my facts - Had them on for the better part of a year on my Bella Donna, and the temperature has NOT changed. I've put in a both stock and lower temp. T-stats and it's been predicatble at both levels. Even with the stock Stat, heat did NOT increase in the engine bay. You're saying the compartment was designed to press air UP into the bay, then to draw it with low pressure OUT of the bay through the grilles. Totally agree with you - very sound idea. However, by introducing ducts to force air DOWN into the engine bay, wouldn't it be possible to create a path for it to exit underneath the vehicle? Seal off the rubber flap's air entrance, and then create an exit at the rear? I've yet to see this rubber flap - recall item?? Or maybe Bella didn't have her slip on when I picked her up for her date  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||09-12-2000||02:30 PM||||Actually, if/when I get a set of Fi-Air-O scoops, I would immediatly add the large rear scoop. Also, the main reason I want the dual scoops is to look into Chris West's dual throtle body design. Kind of like the Indy scoop except not as annoying to get off to open the hood. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||09-12-2000||05:02 PM||||Forcing the air out of the bottom is gonna turn your car into a hovercraft. (Sort of)<P>How about adding a grill to the back of the scoops down by the decklid to let air out, and using ducting to run the cold air into the air filter? You could have the best of both worlds. (if done right, it would make it look even more like a Lambo.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric Marsh||09-12-2000||06:00 PM||||Guys! I'm REALLY interested in checking out these Fi-Air-O Scoops! I've got a V8 that produces major heat in the engine compartment and am working on anything I can do the keep air circulating through there. Where can I find a picture of these scoops? Better yet, where can I get a set?<P>Some people here have been mentioning using them with some scoops on the deck lid. I've already got the scoop - its a fiberglass version of the Challenger T/A scoop mounted backwards. I have not painted or blended it yet. For pics see:<BR> <A HREF="http://home.austin.rr.com/nokidding/hood_scoop/" TARGET=_blank>http://home.austin.rr.com/nokidding/hood_scoop/</A> <P>Some of the pictures might give you an idea why I need to get rid of the heat too.<P>Eric||||reg||11
Z||000009||Will||09-12-2000||07:17 PM||||What a lot of people seem to be overlooking is that before you provide a place for air to go IN, you should provide a place for air to go OUT. That's the oft overlooked basis of circulation. ThatRickGuy and Pontiaddict are on the right rack there.<BR>     You do not want air under the car. Forcing air down creates a high pressure area under the car-- the mortal enemy of aerodynamics and downforce. The Fiero doesn't have much downforce as it is; you don't want to make that worse.<BR>     I think that louvres on the FRONT hood to let air out from behind the radiator at speed with the fan off and at rest with the fan turning would be better for engine cooling than any vent in the rear deck.<BR>     Does the notchback create a low pressure area, or create turbulence and a high pressure area?<BR>     I think that if I still had heat problems after installing hood louvres, I would install a backward facing scoop on the rear decklid, but I would certainly not install those ridiculous looking over the roof scoop you guys are talking about.<P>Eric:<BR>     I've read your webpage and am impressed with your conversion. Have you ever considered the heating water circulation pump from a large bus for your cooling system? I don't know if they all work this way, but some buses have a separate water circuit for interior heat. This circuit is circulated by an electric pump. That might have enough capacity to keep your engine cool, and it almost certainly has more capacity than that Moroso unit you're currently using.<BR>     BTW, why did you carb your engine instead of TPI or EFI of some type?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eric Marsh||09-12-2000||10:55 PM||||Will,<P>I just replaced the Moroso pump with a Meziere pump. It seems to be better, but the car still runs hot. I'm about to stop, take some time, and go over everything point by point.<P>As for the carboration, that was just a budget thing.<P>Eric<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<P>Eric:<BR>     I've read your webpage and am impressed with your conversion. Have you ever considered the heating water circulation pump from a large bus for your cooling system? I don't know if they all work this way, but some buses have a separate water circuit for interior heat. This circuit is circulated by an electric pump. That might have enough capacity to keep your engine cool, and it almost certainly has more capacity than that Moroso unit you're currently using.<BR>     BTW, why did you carb your engine instead of TPI or EFI of some type?[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||6
Z||000011||MikeSchaefer||09-13-2000||01:06 AM||||Hi Bella,<BR>I'm going to stand by what you're saying about the scoops.  They might not be totally functional right now, but they look really good and could be made functional quite easily.  I've seen the idea of putting in a grill on the back of the scoops right above where they connect to the trunk.  This would force tons of air over the scoops and push out more of the hot air quicker.   Here is the car that I saw with the mod (although you can't see it in the pic)  <IMG SRC="http://www.elpaso.net/~schaefer/Motionwc.jpg"> , do you have pictures of yours from behind?  If so, I could easily show you what I mean using photoshop.<BR>Take it easy,<BR>Mike Schaefer <A HREF="http://surf.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://surf.to/fiero</A> ||||reg||6
Z||000012||BellaFiero||09-13-2000||10:30 AM||||Show some manners. Didn't your momma ever teach you that if you don't have something nice to say you shouldn't say anything at all? <BR>It'd be like me saying 'that head on your neck makes you look ridiculous'<P>Not very nice, is it?<BR>I love my car. I like everything I've done to her. I rescued this coupe from the scrap yard, and I completely rebuilt her. It took five Fieros to get her back in running order, and countless more donors from the U-Wrench-Its. Every bolt has my influence, every inch has my touch. I've put so much time and effort into her that my fiance is literally jealous. I've spent nights in the cold and days in utter heat bringing her back from the grave, and you have no right to even attempt to pass judgement on anything that I have decided to do to MY car. <BR>Bad form.<P>Jayse<BR>__________<BR>1988 "Bella Donna" Custom and 1985 GT "Cauldron Born"||||reg||8
Z||000013||BellaFiero||09-13-2000||10:34 AM||||Incidentally, no one has answered my question about how to make the scoops more sleek. Fine. Whatever. All that's been added is "those scoops don't work" and the like. Mike, thanks for the support - I really like them too. I'll have digital pictures on a CD this weekend, so I'll e-mail you some and see what ideas you have. Awesome.<P>Sorry folks. Don't come between a boy and his car. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Songman||09-13-2000||12:16 PM||||Hope you used 100,000 mile sparks plugs in that monster, Eric! It would be a pain in the @ss to have to change them...<P>I would think a lot of heat would come from that spider web of headers. I had to use some custom built jobs like that back in the early 80s when I put a 302 in a Ford Courier California Stepside conversion. <P>Good luck with it<P>Nice job! Lots of ingenuity!<p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 09-13-2000).]||||reg||12
